description:
Juneau AI

Expose your services to AI agents over MCP.

Modules: juneau-rest-server-mcp, juneau-rest-server-mcp-v20250618, juneau-rest-server-mcp-v20260728, juneau-rest-client-mcp, juneau-rest-client-mcp-auth, juneau-rest-client-mcp-v20250618, juneau-rest-client-mcp-v20260728

```java

@Rest(path="/mcp")
public class MyMcpServlet extends McpRestServlet {

    @Override
    protected McpServerConfig createMcpConfig() {
        return new McpServerConfig()
            .setName("my-server").setVersion("1.0.0")
            .addTool(new MyEchoTool());
    }
}
```

- MCP tools, prompts and resources served from a REST resource.
- Two MCP protocol versions, each in its own module.
- An MCP client, with auth.
- Typed beans for the MCP wire messages.
- A [quickstart](/docs/topics/JuneauMcpQuickstart), and a guide to
  [choosing your MCP setup](/docs/topics/JuneauMcpChoosingSetup).

Juneau Bean Query

Search, sort, project and page beans with one query, in memory or in a database.

Modules: juneau-commons, juneau-beanquery-sql, juneau-beanquery-postgres

```java
// Once, at startup.
static final InMemoryBeanQueryContext<Person> PEOPLE = InMemoryBeanQueryContext
    .create(Person.class)
    .exclude("passwordHash")                 // Never searchable, sortable or viewable.
    .guard(p -> ! p.isDeleted())             // Server-only filter a request cannot remove.
    .defaultLimit(50)
    .maxLimit(500)
    .build();

// Per request — binding straight from raw request parameters.
try (InMemoryBeanQuerySession<Person> session = PEOPLE.getSession(people)) {
    BeanQuery query = new BeanQuery()
        .setSearch("age=$gt(21),name=$prefix(Al)")
        .setSort("name,age-")
        .setLimit(20)
        .setOpts("counts=both");
    Page<Person> page = session.find(query);
    page.rows().forEach(this::render);
    page.total().ifPresent(total -> renderFooter(page.matched().orElse(0) + " of " + total));
}
```

- One protocol-agnostic query (search, view, sort, position and limit) over a collection or a SQL table.
- A context-and-session design: the context is built once and shared, and each request opens a session that can
  narrow it but never widen it.
- A server-side guard and limits that a request cannot remove.
- A Postgres dialect in `juneau-beanquery-postgres`.

Juneau Commons

The reflection, collection and settings utilities Juneau is built on.

Modules: juneau-commons

```java

// Wrap a class in ClassInfo
ClassInfo classInfo = ClassInfo.of(MyClass.class);

// Get all methods in parent-to-child order, sorted alphabetically per class
for (MethodInfo methodInfo : classInfo.getAllMethods()) {
    // Do something with it
}

// Get all fields
for (FieldInfo fieldInfo : classInfo.getAllFields()) {
    // Do something with it
}
```

- Cached reflection through `ClassInfo`, `MethodInfo`, `FieldInfo` and their relatives.
- Collection, conversion, IO, time and concurrency utilities.
- `Settings`, with layered property sources.
- The SVL variable resolver (`$E`, `$S` and others).
- Injection.

Juneau Console

Browser admin consoles built from your REST resources.

Modules: juneau-console, juneau-console-freemarker, juneau-console-views, juneau-console-views-markdown, juneau-console-widgets, juneau-console-datatables, juneau-console-bus, juneau-console-bus-websocket, juneau-rest-server-view-jsp, juneau-rest-server-view-thymeleaf, juneau-rest-server-view-mustache, juneau-rest-server-view-freemarker

```java

@Rest(
    path="/console",
    mixins={
        FreemarkerMixin.class,        // renders .ftlh views
        ConsoleChromeMixin.class,     // chrome.css, theme CSS, juneau-console.js
        ViewsMixin.class,             // the views toolkit scripts and CSS
        DataTablesMixin.class,        // juneau-datatables.js
        WebJarsMixin.class            // /webjars/*: jQuery and DataTables
    }
)
public class PetConsole extends BasicRestServlet {

    @Bean
    public FreemarkerMixin freemarker() {
        // Typed as FreemarkerMixin: the return type is load-bearing.
        return ConsoleFreemarkerMixin.create()
            .basePath("/templates/")
            .chromeTemplate("console/chrome.ftlh")
            .build();
    }

    @RestGet(path="/pets")
    public View pets() {
        return FreemarkerView.of("console/pets.ftlh");
    }
}
```

- Themeable, server-rendered console chrome: header, navigation tree and footer.
- Rich table views with search, sort, paging and row actions.
- Chart cards, the Terminal view and Terminal regions.
- Job-log cards, row jobs and async jobs over the console message bus.
- Declarative widgets and Markdown.
- JSP, Thymeleaf, Mustache and FreeMarker views.
- No front-end build. [Build a console page](/docs/topics/BuildingAConsolePage) step by step.

Juneau Marshall

One bean. Every format.

Modules: juneau-marshall, juneau-marshall-rdf

```java

public class Person {
    public String name = "John Smith";
    public int age = 21;
}

String json = Json.of(new Person());
String xml  = Xml.of(new Person());
String yaml = Yaml.of(new Person());

Person back = Json.to(json, Person.class);
```

```json
{"age":21,"name":"John Smith"}
```

```xml
<object><age>21</age><name>John Smith</name></object>
```

```yaml
age: 21
name: John Smith
```

- One API for 38 formats, each both serialized and parsed: JSON, JSON5, XML, HTML, YAML, CSV, UON, MessagePack, TOML, Protobuf and more, including 12 RDF syntaxes (`juneau-marshall-rdf`).
- One annotation model (`@BeanType`, `@BeanProp`) across every format.
- Round-trip parsing back to the same beans.
- Swaps for types you can't annotate.
- No required dependencies: `juneau-marshall` needs only `juneau-commons`.

Juneau REST Client

Call REST APIs through typed Java interfaces.

Modules: juneau-rest-client, juneau-rest-client-apache-httpclient-45, juneau-rest-client-apache-httpclient-50, juneau-rest-client-jetty, juneau-rest-client-okhttp, juneau-rest-client-classic

```java

@Remote(path="/pets")
public interface PetStore {

    @RemoteGet("/{id}")
    Pet getPet(@Path("id") long id);
}

RestClient client = RestClient.builder()
    .serializer(Json5Serializer.DEFAULT)
    .parser(Json5Parser.DEFAULT)
    .rootUrl("https://api.example.com")
    .build();

PetStore store = client.remote(PetStore.class);
Pet pet = store.getPet(123);
```

- A fluent client that maps request and response bodies to POJOs.
- Remote proxies: call a REST API through a plain Java interface.
- Pluggable transports: Java’s built-in `HttpClient` by default, or Apache HttpClient 4.5 and 5, Jetty and OkHttp.
- Tests without a server through `juneau-rest-mock`.
- The [classic client](/docs/topics/JuneauRestClient), built on Apache HttpClient, for existing code.

Juneau REST Server

Annotate a POJO and serve it as REST in every format Juneau speaks.

Modules: juneau-rest-server, juneau-rest-common, juneau-microservice, juneau-microservice-jetty, juneau-microservice-tomcat, juneau-rest-server-springboot, juneau-rest-mock, juneau-rest-server-auth-jwt, juneau-rest-server-auth-oauth, juneau-rest-server-auth-oidc-rp, juneau-rest-server-auth-saml, juneau-rest-server-metrics-micrometer, juneau-rest-server-tracing-otel

```java

@Rest(path="/pets")
public class PetStore extends BasicRestServlet {

    @RestGet("/{id}")
    public Pet get(@Path("id") long id) {
        return store.getPet(id);
    }
}
```

- Content negotiation across every Juneau format, from the request's `Accept` and `Content-Type` headers.
- Generated OpenAPI and Swagger documentation for every resource.
- Runs on Jetty, Tomcat or Spring Boot, or as a config-driven microservice.
- Auth modules for JWT, OAuth, OIDC and SAML.
- Metrics and tracing through Micrometer and OpenTelemetry.
- Serverless tests with `juneau-rest-mock`.

# Release 10.0.0: Marshall, commons and config

This page is part of the [Release 10.0.0](/docs/release-notes/10.0.0) release notes. The overview page lists the major changes, the breaking changes and the API changes for the whole release.

## juneau-marshall / juneau-commons

### View-based property projection (`@MarshalledProp(view=...)`)

Juneau now supports **view-based projection**: declare named views on bean properties and select an active
view per serialize/parse call so only the in-view properties are emitted or consumed.  This is the Juneau
equivalent of Jackson's `@JsonView` / `MapperFeature.DEFAULT_VIEW_INCLUSION`.

Tag a property with one or more string view names via the new `view` member on `@MarshalledProp`:

```java
public class Person {
    public String id;                         // untagged — visible in every view

    @MarshalledProp(view = "summary")
    public String name;

    @MarshalledProp(view = "detail")
    public String description;
}
```

Activate a view on any serializer or parser builder, or override it per call on a session:

```java
// Context-level default:
var s = Json5Serializer.DEFAULT.copy().activeView("summary").build();
s.write(person);  // → {id:'1',name:'Alice'}

// Per-call session override:
s.createSession().activeView("detail").build().write(person);  // → {description:'A person',id:'1'}
```

Key behaviours:
- **Default-view policy:** untagged properties are included in all views by default (mirrors Jackson's
  `DEFAULT_VIEW_INCLUSION=true`). Call `disableDefaultViewInclusion()` to reverse.
- **Union semantics:** `@MarshalledProp(view = {"A","B"})` makes a property visible when `A` *or* `B` is active.
- **Flat string names:** no marker-class hierarchy required.
- **Parse-side:** out-of-view input properties are routed through the existing unknown/ignored-property
  mechanism (silently ignored by default; `ignoreUnknownBeanProperties(false)` makes them throw).
- **Mix-in support:** view membership can be applied to ...

# Release Notes 8.x

This page collects every release in the 8.x line, newest first.


## Release 8.2.0 {#release-8-2-0}

**Date:** Oct 14, 2020

Juneau 8.2.0 is a major release. 

The most significant change is the addition of an entirely new  ~~RestClient~~ API build from scratch with near 100% unit test coverage.

The new API is located in the `org.apache.juneau.rest.client2` package and will replace the old API in 9.0.

### juneau-marshall

- <a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/annotation/Bean.html" target="_blank">@Bean</a> and <a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/annotation/BeanIgnore.html" target="_blank">@BeanIgnore</a> annotations can alternately occur in parent class hierarchy.
The first one found dictates whether a class is ignored as a bean or not.

- Applying the <a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/annotation/Bean.html" target="_blank">@Bean</a> annotation on a class will now force non-public classes to be interpreted as beans.
  For example, applying <a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/annotation/Bean.html" target="_blank">@Bean</a> to a `private` class will force it to be treated as a bean.
  Also, if a public bean constructor cannot be found, the default constructor will be used regardless of it's visibility if the <a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/annotation/Bean.html" target="_blank">@Bean</a> annotation is on the class.

- The `@Beanc` annotation can now be recognized and used on non-public constructors.

- Annotations are now aggregated across the entire class hierarchy instead of simply being overridden.

  The following is an example.

  ```java
  // Parent class with properties a,b,c
  @Bean(bpi="a,b,c")
  public class MyClass {
  public int a, b, c, d;
  }

  // New behavior:  Child class with properties a,c because @Beans are aggregated.
  // Old behavior:  Child class with properties a,c,d because @Bean is overridden.
  @Bean(bpx="b")
  public class MyChildClass extends MyClass {
  public int a, b, c, d;
  }
  `...

With `juneau-marshall` on the classpath (the project from the [previous page](/docs/topics/GettingStartedInstall)
has it), the `Json` marshaller turns a bean into JSON and back in one call.

Create the bean, `src/main/java/com/example/demo/Pet.java`. The REST pages reuse it:

```java
package com.example.demo;

public class Pet {
    public int id;
    public String name;

    public Pet() {}
    public Pet(int id, String name) { this.id = id; this.name = name; }
}
```

Then create `src/main/java/com/example/demo/Main.java`:

```java
package com.example.demo;

import org.apache.juneau.marshall.json.JsonSerializer;
import org.apache.juneau.marshall.marshaller.Json;
import org.apache.juneau.marshall.marshaller.Json5;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String json = Json.of(new Pet(1, "Fido"));           // {"id":1,"name":"Fido"}
        System.out.println(json);

        Pet pet = Json.to(json, Pet.class);                  // back to a bean
        System.out.println(pet.name);                        // Fido

        System.out.println(Json5.of(new Pet(1, "Fido")));    // {id:1,name:'Fido'}

        JsonSerializer serializer = JsonSerializer.create().sq().build();
        System.out.println(serializer.write(new Pet(1, "Fido")));   // {'id':1,'name':'Fido'}
    }
}
```

Run it from the project directory:

```bash
mvn compile exec:java -Dexec.mainClass=com.example.demo.Main
```

Expected output:

```
{"id":1,"name":"Fido"}
Fido
{id:1,name:'Fido'}
{'id':1,'name':'Fido'}
```

Import the marshaller classes by name, as above. A wildcard import of both `org.apache.juneau.marshall.json.*`
and `org.apache.juneau.marshall.marshaller.*` makes `Json` ambiguous, because both packages have a class of
that name.

## Other formats

Each format has a marshaller with the same `of(...)` / `to(...)` methods. `Json5` (used in `Main` above) writes the relaxed,
quote-free JSON dialect, which is handy for logs and tests.

For XML, HTM...

A Juneau REST resource is a plain class annotated with `@Rest`. Methods annotated with `@RestGet`,
`@RestPost`, and friends become endpoints, and return values are serialized according to the client's
`Accept` header.

See the guide: [Build a REST API on Spring Boot](/docs/guides/SpringBootRestApi)

Create `src/main/java/com/example/demo/PetResource.java`. `Pet` is the bean from the
[previous page](/docs/topics/GettingStartedSerializer).

```java
package com.example.demo;

import org.apache.juneau.http.*;
import org.apache.juneau.rest.server.*;
import org.apache.juneau.rest.server.servlet.*;

@Rest(path="/pets", title="Pet service")
public class PetResource extends BasicRestServlet {

    @RestGet("/{id}")
    public Pet getPet(@Path("id") int id) {
        return new Pet(id, "Fido");
    }
}
```

The same method answers `Accept: application/json`, `text/xml`, `text/html` (a browsable page), and the
other formats registered on the resource, with no extra code.

## Servlet or Resource? {#servlet-or-resource}

:::tip
`BasicRestServlet` is an `HttpServlet`, so it can be a top-level resource in any servlet container or in the
Jetty microservice. `BasicRestResource` is the same resource without the servlet base class: use it for child
resources (`@Rest(children=...)`) and for Spring Boot beans. See
[REST Children](/docs/topics/JuneauRestServer#rest-children) and
[Spring Boot REST API](/docs/guides/SpringBootRestApi).
:::

## Run it

The Jetty microservice bundle in your `pom.xml` includes an embedded Jetty server. Create
`src/main/java/com/example/demo/App.java` to start it with `PetResource` as the root resource:

```java
package com.example.demo;

import org.apache.juneau.microservice.jetty.*;

public class App {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        JettyMicroservice.run(args, new PetResource(), false).join();
    }
}
```

The server writes its request log to a `logs` directory in the working directory, creating it if needed.
Start t...

The REST client calls the service from the [previous page](/docs/topics/GettingStartedRestApp) and parses
the response into the same `Pet` bean. With that server still running, create
`src/main/java/com/example/demo/Client.java`:

```java
// Artifact: juneau-rest-client
package com.example.demo;

import org.apache.juneau.marshall.json5.*;
import org.apache.juneau.rest.client.*;

public class Client {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        try (RestClient client = RestClient.builder()
                .rootUrl("http://localhost:10000")
                .serializer(Json5Serializer.create().build())
                .parser(Json5Parser.create().build())
                .build()) {

            try (var resp = client.get("/pets/{id}").pathData("id", 1).run()) {
                resp.assertOk();
                Pet pet = resp.body().as(Pet.class);
                System.out.println(pet.id + " " + pet.name);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Run it from a second terminal in the project directory:

```bash
mvn compile exec:java -Dexec.mainClass=com.example.demo.Client
```

It prints `1 Fido`, followed by a log line for the request.

Always close the response (it holds the connection) and close the client at shutdown; the `try` blocks above do both.

:::tip Which client?
`juneau-rest-client` (`org.apache.juneau.rest.client.RestClient`) is the recommended REST client. It is generally
available in 10.0.0, transport-agnostic, and uses the JDK `HttpClient` by default. `juneau-rest-client-classic`
(`org.apache.juneau.rest.client.classic.RestClient`, Apache HttpClient 4.5) stays supported and is not deprecated; it is
the stable choice for existing users. See [Choosing a REST Client](/docs/topics/ChoosingARestClient).
:::

## A typed proxy instead

For services you call often, declare an interface and let Juneau implement it. See
[Declarative Remote-Proxy Features](/docs/topics/NextGenRestClient#declarative-remote-proxy-features-next-gen).

## Lear...

The [org.apache.juneau.commons.collections](pathname:///site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/collections/package-summary.html) package provides enhanced collection utilities that extend the standard Java Collections Framework with fluent APIs, specialized collection types, and caching capabilities.

## Key Classes

### Collection Builders

### <java-class><a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/collections/Lists.html" target="_blank">Lists</a></java-class>
Fluent builder for constructing `List` instances with various configuration options.

```java
import static org.apache.juneau.commons.utils.CollectionUtils.*;

// Basic usage
List<String> list = listBuilder(String.class)
    .add("apple", "banana", "cherry")
    .build();

// With sorting
List<Integer> sorted = listBuilder(Integer.class)
    .add(3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6)
    .sorted()
    .build();

// Conditional elements
List<String> filtered = listBuilder(String.class)
    .add("always")
    .addIf(includeOptional, "optional")
    .build();

// Immutable list
List<String> immutable = listBuilder(String.class)
    .add("read", "only")
    .unmodifiable()
    .build();
```

### <java-class><a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/collections/Maps.html" target="_blank">Maps</a></java-class>
Fluent builder for constructing `Map` instances.

```java
import static org.apache.juneau.commons.utils.CollectionUtils.*;

// Basic usage
Map<String, Integer> map = mapBuilder(String.class, Integer.class)
    .add("one", 1)
    .add("two", 2)
    .add("three", 3)
    .build();

// Using pairs
Map<String, String> props = mapBuilder(String.class, String.class)
    .addPairs("host", "localhost", "port", "8080")
    .build();

// With sorting by key
Map<String, Integer> sorted = mapBuilder(String.class, Integer.class)
    .add("zebra", 3)
    .add("apple", 1)
    .add("banana", 2)
    .sorted()
    .build();
```

### <java-class><a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/collections/Sets.html" target="_b...

The [org.apache.juneau.commons.lang](pathname:///site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/lang/package-summary.html) package provides language utilities including value wrappers, version management, string formatting, state enums, and other common language constructs.

## Key Classes

### <java-class><a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/lang/Holder.html" target="_blank">Holder</a></java-class>
A generic mutable value wrapper.

```java
// Basic usage
Holder<String> name = Holder.of("John");
name.set("Jane");
String result = name.get();  // Returns "Jane"

// Use in lambda (effectively final variable)
Holder<String> result = Holder.empty();
list.forEach(x -> {
    if (x.matches(criteria)) {
        result.set(x);
    }
});

// With default values
Holder<String> optional = Holder.empty();
String value = optional.orElse("default");  // Returns "default"

// Mapping values
Holder<Integer> number = Holder.of(5);
Holder<String> text = number.map(Object::toString);  // Holder of "5"

// With listener for change notification
Holder<String> monitored = Holder.of("initial")
    .listener(newValue -> log("Value changed to: " + newValue));
monitored.set("updated");  // Triggers listener
```

### Specialized Holder Classes

### <java-class><a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/lang/IntegerHolder.html" target="_blank">IntegerHolder</a></java-class>
Mutable integer with additional convenience methods.

```java
IntegerHolder counter = IntegerHolder.of(0);
counter.increment();  // 1
counter.decrement();  // 0
int value = counter.getAndIncrement();  // Returns 0, then sets to 1
```

### <java-class><a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/lang/LongHolder.html" target="_blank">LongHolder</a></java-class>
Mutable long with additional convenience methods.

```java
LongHolder counter = LongHolder.of(0L);
counter.increment();  // 1L
counter.decrement();  // 0L
long value = counter.getAndIncrement();  // Returns 0L, then sets to 1L
```

### <java-class><a hre...

The [org.apache.juneau.commons.reflect](pathname:///site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/reflect/package-summary.html) package provides comprehensive reflection utilities for working with classes, methods, fields, constructors, and annotations in a more convenient and type-safe way than the standard Java reflection APIs.

## Key Classes

### <java-class><a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/reflect/ReflectionUtils.html" target="_blank">ReflectionUtils</a></java-class>
Utility class providing convenient static methods for creating reflection info objects.

```java
import static org.apache.juneau.commons.reflect.ReflectionUtils.*;

// Convert Class to ClassInfo
ClassInfo ci = info(MyClass.class);

// Convert Method to MethodInfo
Method m = MyClass.class.getMethod("myMethod");
MethodInfo mi = info(m);

// Convert Field to FieldInfo
Field f = MyClass.class.getField("myField");
FieldInfo fi = info(f);

// Convert Constructor to ConstructorInfo
Constructor<?> c = MyClass.class.getConstructor();
ConstructorInfo ci2 = info(c);
```

### <java-class><a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/reflect/ClassInfo.html" target="_blank">ClassInfo</a></java-class>
Lightweight utility class for introspecting information about a class.

```java
// Wrap a class in ClassInfo
ClassInfo classInfo = ClassInfo.of(MyClass.class);

// Get all methods in parent-to-child order, sorted alphabetically per class
for (MethodInfo methodInfo : classInfo.getAllMethods()) {
    // Do something with it
}

// Get all class-level annotations in parent-to-child order
for (MyAnnotation annotation : classInfo.getAnnotations(MyAnnotation.class)) {
    // Do something with it
}

// Get all fields
for (FieldInfo fieldInfo : classInfo.getAllFields()) {
    // Do something with it
}

// Get all constructors
for (ConstructorInfo constructorInfo : classInfo.getConstructors()) {
    // Do something with it
}
```

### <java-class><a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/reflect/MethodInf...

The [org.apache.juneau.commons.bean](pathname:///site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/bean/package-summary.html) package provides Juneau's bean-modeling layer — the annotations and introspection engine that describe how Java beans are recognized, read, written, and mapped, independently of any particular marshalling format. The marshalling modules build on this layer through a small set of SPI seams so the bean model stays decoupled from serialization concerns.

## Bean Modeling Annotations

Annotations that control how classes and their properties are introspected.

### <java-annotation><a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/bean/BeanType.html" target="_blank">BeanType</a></java-annotation>
Applied to a class to control bean introspection and modeling — property inclusion/order, read-only/write-only properties, a custom `PropertyNamer`, a stop class, and more.

```java
@BeanType(properties="id,name,city", propertyNamer=PropertyNamerDLC.class)
public class Address {
    public String id, name, city;
}
```

### <java-annotation><a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/bean/BeanProp.html" target="_blank">BeanProp</a></java-annotation>
Applied to fields, getters/setters, or constructor parameters to control property modeling — most commonly to override the property name via its `value()`/`name()`.

```java fragment
public class Person {
    @BeanProp("full-name")
    public String getName() { ... }
}
```

### <java-annotation><a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/bean/BeanCtor.html" target="_blank">BeanCtor</a></java-annotation>
Maps constructor arguments to property names on beans with read-only properties.

```java
public class Point {
    public final int x, y;

    @BeanCtor(properties="x,y")
    public Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
}
```

