In the section [Swaps](/docs/topics/Swaps), you were introduced to annotations that can be applied to bean classes, methods,
fields, and constructors such as <a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/bean/BeanType.html" target="_blank">@BeanType</a>:

```java fragment
// Address class with only street/city/state properties (in that order).
// All other properties are ignored.
@BeanType(properties="street,city,state")
public class Address { ... }
```

An alternate way of applying these annotations is to attach them to unrelated classes and methods and then tell your
serializer or parser where to find them.  Each annotation pairs with a sibling `*Apply` annotation that separates
the targeting (`on()`/`onClass()`) from the content (`value()`).

```java fragment
// Unannotated class.
public class Address { ... }

@BeanTypeApply(onClass=Address.class, value=@BeanType(properties="street,city,state"))
public static class DummyClass {}

WriterSerializer serializer = JsonSerializer
    .create()
    .applyAnnotations(DummyClass.class)
    .build();

String json = serializer.write(addressBean);
```

The advantage to this approach is it allows you to use Juneau annotations on classes/methods/fields/constructors where
you might not have access to the source code, or when you only want to selectively apply the annotation under certain
scenarios instead of globally.

For example, the following shows the @BeanTypeApply annotation being selectively applied on a single REST method (described later
in [juneau-rest-server](/docs/topics/JuneauRestServer)):

```java
@RestGet
@BeanTypeApply(onClass=Address.class, value=@BeanType(properties="street,city,state"))
public List getAddresses() {}
```

