Juneau 10.0 introduces a unified property-source model behind
<a href="/site/apidocs/org/apache/juneau/commons/settings/Settings.html" target="_blank">Settings</a>.

## When to use it

Use a property source when configuration values should come from somewhere the defaults do not cover, such as a database or a secrets service, yet still resolve through `Settings`, `Config` and the `$P{...}` SVL variable.

## Example

```java
import java.util.*;

import org.apache.juneau.commons.settings.*;

public class PropertySourceExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // A custom source backed by a map.
        Map<String,String> values = Map.of("app.greeting", "hello");
        Settings.get().addSource(FunctionalPropertySource.of(values::get));

        // The source is now part of the default stack.
        String greeting = Settings.get().get("app.greeting").asOptional().orElse("none");
        System.out.println(greeting);
    }
}
```

## Options

| Type | Role |
|---|---|
| `PropertySource` | Read-only source of `name -> Optional<String>` |
| `PropertyStore` | Read/write source (extends `PropertySource`) |
| `PropertySourceProvider` | ServiceLoader SPI for auto-registering ambient sources |

## Default source stack

By default, the stack resolves in this priority order (highest first):

1. Thread-local overrides (`Settings.setLocal(...)`)
2. Global overrides (`Settings.setGlobal(...)`)
3. Sources added at runtime via `addSource(...)`, newest first
4. `Config` (SPI priority 100)
5. CLI args (`ArgsPropertySource`, 50)
6. JVM system properties (40)
7. Environment variables, verbatim name (30)
8. `.env` file (20)
9. Manifest entries (10)
10. Relaxed environment variable names

If no registered source supplies system properties, a fallback system-property source sits just above the relaxed environment source. The environment, `.env`, manifest and args sources cache their lookups; system properties and runtime-added sources are read liv...

Configuration change events can be listened for with `Config.addListener(ConfigEventListener)`.

## When to use it

Use a listener to react when an entry changes, either through `Config.commit()` or because the file changed on the file system, for example to reload a cache.

## Example

```java
import org.apache.juneau.config.*;
import org.apache.juneau.config.event.*;
import org.apache.juneau.config.store.*;

public class ConfigListenerExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        MemoryStore store = MemoryStore.create().build();
        store.update("MyConfig.cfg", "[MySection]\nkey1 = a\n");
        Config config = Config.create("MyConfig.cfg").store(store).build();

        // Add a listener for changes to MySection/key1
        config.addListener(events -> {
            for (ConfigEvent event : events) {
                if (event.getType() == ConfigEventType.SET_ENTRY && "MySection".equals(event.getSection()) && "key1".equals(event.getKey()))
                    System.out.println("key1 changed to " + event.getValue());
            }
        });

        config.set("MySection/key1", "b");
        config.commit();
    }
}
```

